\page\par\b Build 468 behavior update\b0\par
Build 468 - historical Tag-window cancellation and main-window recovery. In Build 468, if Quit was selected while the then-existing GUI Tag window was actively processing, TLO requested cooperative cancellation and closed that Tag window. Tag, Add Shows, and Inventory remain disabled only while the existing tag worker is still stopping. When the worker exits, the main window automatically recalculates its action states and returns to normal idle operation. No additional Inventory run is required solely to restore the controls.\par
\page\par
\b Build 469 behavior update\b0\par
Build 469 - Main-window Tag and control cleanup. GUI Tag no longer opens a separate Tag window; it uses the main Path(s), Slam, selected tag mode, and current main-window settings, with progress/output in the main Inventory window. Keep and report now greys/disables Folder removal and Folder corruption threshold and uses effective folder removal Never. If SETLISTFM_API_KEY is missing or blank, both setlist.fm controls are unchecked and disabled. If the normal key is present but SETLISTFMUPGRADE_API_KEY is missing or blank, only setlist.fm upgrade is unchecked and disabled. Max Workers is an independent ceiling rather than a forced worker count; phases may use fewer workers because of Performance Mode, physical-drive serialization, work-group counts, or correctness constraints. The View Issues horizontal scrollbar now has real overflow for long Explanation/Path values. Successful parser-selection decisions are informational rather than tagging warnings; consequential warnings and errors remain visible.\par
\par
\b Build 470 behavior update\b0\par
Build 470 - unidentifiedShows.txt status cleanup. Normal postprocess messages that create or update unidentifiedShows.txt, including a completion result of zero unidentified shows, are informational housekeeping and are not counted or displayed as GUI warnings. Actual unidentified-show conditions continue to appear in View Issues and the appropriate logs.\par
\par
\b Build 471 behavior update\b0\par
Build 471 - exact-case Rename Compliantly. The compliant folder name now includes the exact canonical capitalization resolved by TLO. A folder such as dave van ronk 1978-02-17 Main Point Bryn Mawr, PA is renamed to Dave Van Ronk 1978-02-17 Main Point Bryn Mawr, PA when Rename Compliantly is selected. On case-insensitive filesystems TLO uses a unique temporary sibling as an intermediate rename when the two spellings identify the same directory entry; this does not create an alternate/copy suffix. Full Inventory/GUI or standalone Tag and Add Shows use the same rule.\par
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1{\loch
Build 472: Auto update now asks Yes or Skip before downloading. Yes downloads the verified update ZIP to Downloads and returns; Skip returns without downloading. Complete distributions include a blank header-only bootlist.csv starter, while update ZIPs continue to omit bootlist.csv.}\par
\b Build 473 behavior update\b0\par
Build 473 - unknown-date descriptor fallback. When a show or collection has Date xxxx-xx-xx and TLO has no Venue or Location, TLO may add a separate descriptive label instead of inventing geographic metadata. It looks only at conservative evidence such as explicit Collection/Release/Session/Broadcast-style setlist headers, quoted or decorated release titles, a title-like header separated from the track list, a descriptive setlist filename, or cleaned folder-name residue. Song rows, technical lineage, disc/set labels, generic housekeeping text, and prose are rejected. A successful descriptor produces a Show name such as Pink Floyd xxxx-xx-xx Early Flights and is recorded in the metadata log as DESCRIPTOR and DESCRIPTOR_SOURCE. Venue and Location remain blank. If no safe descriptor is found, TLO keeps the existing Artist xxxx-xx-xx naming.\par
\par
\b Build 474 behavior update\b0\par
The main Add Shows button is now labeled Add New Shows on two lines. This is a label-only change; the existing Add Shows/Updater behavior is unchanged.\par
The main GUI no longer has a Slam (optional) text box. Use the toBeInventoried-style syntax directly in Path(s), including --$slam on the applicable entry when needed. Multiple Path(s) entries are separated by semicolons. Commas are ordinary path/directive characters and do not require quotes; quotes remain accepted. Examples:\line
C:\\Boots\\Grateful Dead\line
C:\\Boots\\RTF Paris --$slam Return to Forever\line
C:\\Boots\\Van Morrison 1994-12-03 Dublin, Ireland;D:\\Incoming --$copy E:\\TLOCopies\line
C:\\Lists\\next-run.txt\par
The last example loads a .txt control file whose non-comment lines use the same [Volume], --$slam, --$copy, and --$copy-delete syntax. Blank lines, # comments, and REM comments are ignored in control files. Native-Windows drag/drop into Path(s) also uses semicolons. Commas inside dropped paths are preserved normally and do not require quotes.\par
The main action buttons are horizontally more compact. Manual Tweaks opens a Manual Updates window with Original (to be edited), New Name, Update Tags, Save, and Quit. Original accepts one folder or one .txt file. New Name accepts typed text or one dropped folder and TLO keeps only that folder's leaf name; the drop no longer depends on Original already containing a valid folder. When Original is a .txt file, New Name is disabled. Update Tags is checked by default.\par
For a single folder, Save renames the Original folder in its existing parent when necessary, matches the canonical bootlist path without crossing to a same-named folder on another volume, and then adds the New Name identity and replacement exported setlist. If another bootlist row still uses the old Show name, TLO preserves that shared exported setlist family instead of deleting it. If the folder was already renamed to the requested New Name in the same parent, Save accepts that state and repairs the inventory. TLO preserves the existing bootlist volume and parent path and changes only the old leaf folder name. The New Name is the authoritative Show name for this manual correction.\par
For a .txt Original, use blank lines, # comments, and REM comments exactly as in toBeInventoried.txt. Every data line must be:\line
Path ----> Folder Name\line
Example:\line
# Correct two inventory entries\line
C:\\Boots\\dave van ronk 1978-02-17 Main Point Bryn Mawr, PA ----> Dave Van Ronk 1978-02-17 Main Point Bryn Mawr, PA\line
REM already renamed on disk; repair inventory/setlist identity\line
"D:\\Boots\\Artist, City, ST" ----> Artist xxxx-xx-xx Collection Title\par
Save processes each valid line independently and reports any per-line failures.\par
\par
\b Build 475 behavior update - v1.7\b0\par
Build 475 advances TLO to public version v1.7 while continuing the same build-number sequence. Manual Updates adds recovery for an Original that is no longer in bootlist.csv but is still listed in TLOHome/unidentifiedShows.txt. TLO first prefers an exact unresolved path match; otherwise the Original leaf folder name must match exactly one unidentified-show path. Ambiguous leaf-only matches are not changed.\par
When this unidentified-show case is found, TLO opens a window titled Where is the unidentified show going? with the normal TLO header and one Path box. You can type/paste a single folder path or drag/drop one folder on native Windows. This path is only location information; TLO does not move or rename anything in this branch because the physical move/rename is assumed to have already been done manually.\par
Path may be either the destination parent or the already-renamed New Name folder itself. Example: if New Name is Dave Van Ronk 1978-02-17 Main Point Bryn Mawr, PA, dropping D:\\Boots\\Dave Van Ronk 1978-02-17 Main Point Bryn Mawr, PA causes TLO to strip the New Name leaf and use D:\\Boots as the parent. Dropping D:\\Boots directly has the same result. TLO inventories D:\\Boots\\Dave Van Ronk 1978-02-17 Main Point Bryn Mawr, PA as it exists on disk.\par
After a successful Save, TLO removes the matched Original line from unidentifiedShows.txt, reads the final New Name folder, writes the exported setlist using New Name as the authoritative Show identity, and writes the destination to bootlist.csv in the normal [Volume] rootless-path format. If the final folder is not present or the inventory update fails, TLO does not silently discard the unresolved-show state.\par
For a .txt Manual Updates batch, unidentified-show lines use the same rule. TLO asks for each required destination Path before applying the batch. Canceling a required destination prompt aborts the batch before changes begin. Inventory-backed lines continue to use the normal Manual Updates rename/repair behavior.\par

\par
\b Build 476 behavior update\b0\par
Build 476 restores semicolons as the separator between entries in Path(s). Commas and apostrophes/single quotes are ordinary characters inside paths and directive values and do not require quotes. Matching double quotes remain accepted. Native-Windows drag/drop appends multiple folders/control files with semicolons and does not add quotes merely because a path contains a comma. All Build 475 Manual Updates behavior is unchanged.\par
\b Build 478 behavior update\b0\par
Build 478 treats apostrophes/single quotes as ordinary text in Path(s), so names such as Tito Puente's cannot trigger an unmatched-quote error; optional explicit quoting uses matching double quotes. Manual Updates adds an Update Tags checkbox that is checked by default. If checked, Save updates the affected folder tags before finalizing the setlist/bootlist inventory update; if unchecked, it leaves tags unchanged. The checkbox applies to single-folder saves, unidentified-show recovery, and .txt batches. New Name accepts a single dropped folder independently of Original except while a .txt batch is selected. Rename Compliantly may also adopt authoritative online capitalization when venue/location text is identical except for case, without allowing substantive metadata replacement.\par
\par
\b Build 479 stabilization update\b0\par
Build 479 fixes GUI Tag completion and main-button recovery after Tag finishes. Manual Updates now compares physical Original paths with canonical rootless bootlist paths, never substitutes a same-leaf folder on another volume, preserves a shared old setlist family while another row still uses it, writes unidentified-show recovery rows in canonical [Volume] rootless-path form, and atomically replaces bootlist.csv and unidentifiedShows.txt.\par
When Update Tags is checked, a parseable New Name supplies the user-directed Artist/Album identity. Manual Updates never converts SHN/SHNF files. Tag writes occur before the inventory commit and are not rolled back; a later failure warns that tags may already have changed. setlist.fm results from another artist are ignored. Online capitalization only improves lower/all-uppercase local text to useful mixed case and rebuilds Location when appropriate. Definite future dates are rejected; two-digit years use the latest non-future century. Semicolon-containing dropped Path(s) values are automatically double-quoted; single quotes and apostrophes remain literal text.\par
\par
\b Build 480 requirements/MP3 metadata update\b0\par
Build 480 consolidates the current behavior rules so stale requirements no longer conflict with the shipped code. unidentifiedShows.txt stays in encounter order; a nonblank Show Name, rather than a nonblank Artist by itself, controls whether a supported record can reach bootlist output; normal setlist.fm remains an eTreeDB fallback while Thorough Setlist Matching may query it proactively for corroboration; current version text comes from the shared version module; and GUI Tag uses Path(s) with inline --$slam rather than a separate Slam or Tagging Path field. tlo-ggi no longer accepts --tag-path; that option belongs only to standalone tlo-tag.\par
In non-compliant inventory, metadata tag sampling now treats MP3 as an identification peer of FLAC. Up to two supported FLAC/MP3 files may contribute ALBUMARTIST, ARTIST, ALBUM, and DATE evidence. Compliant mode continues to ignore audio metadata tags. Artist evidence follows the canonical precedence described above, so sampled tags cannot displace stronger path/setlist evidence. State/location parsing also rejects mixed-case two-letter words such as In as non-terminal state-code anchors; an uppercase state code or full state name followed by source/recording text may still anchor the location and preserves the trailing text as one parenthetical when the left side yields a valid city/venue split.\par
\par
\b Build 481 security hardening\b0\par
TLO now treats collection text, tag values, recovery journals, symbolic links, generated delete scripts, and spreadsheet output more defensively. Machine-parsed logs encode embedded control characters so a tag or online error cannot create a second log instruction. Fallback setlist reads are restricted to local regular files associated with the show and use bounded readers.\par
Interrupted sibling-collection recovery now proceeds automatically only when the recovery journal is registered to the active TLOHome. An unregistered or malformed journal stops Inventory without moving or deleting anything. Do not manually delete a recovery container or journal when TLO reports this condition.\par
Tag Copy refuses folders containing symbolic links. Sibling setlist aggregation ignores symlinked setlist files. Convert shn uses a securely allocated temporary FLAC file and will not overwrite an existing or dangling-link destination.\par
Windows duplicate-cleanup batch files safely quote percent characters. The Issues window does not launch arbitrary files or network paths; local files are revealed through their containing folder. bootlist.csv protects values that spreadsheet programs could otherwise interpret as formulas while preserving the original value when TLO reads the file. Compliant folder names also avoid reserved Windows device names such as CON, NUL, COM1-COM9, and LPT1-LPT9.\par
\par
\b Build 482 CI compatibility correction\b0\par
Build 482 contains no user-visible application behavior change. It corrects the Manual Updates regression fixtures used by the release build so they store bootlist paths in the same canonical [Volume] rootless-path form produced by Full Inventory. This keeps the Build 481 cross-volume safety behavior intact while making the tests independent of Linux filesystem volume labels.\par
\par
\b Build 483 setlist.fm upgrade environment gate\b0\par
Build 483 adds SETLISTFMUPGRADE_API_KEY as a separate gate for setlist.fm upgrade; set it to the same value as SETLISTFM_API_KEY. Without it, normal setlist.fm still works but upgrade is greyed out, and command-line upgrade requests are rejected.\par


\par
\b Build 485 persistent Copy Requests\b0\par
Build 485 introduced Copy Requests for copying a requested subset of the collection over multiple sessions and changing sets of connected source drives. Build 486 moves the Copy Requests entry point from the main action-row buttons to the upper-right hamburger menu. A Copy Request stays saved under TLOHome even after TLO is closed, so you can connect one group of source drives, copy everything available from them, disconnect those drives, connect another group days later, and choose Continue without starting over.\par
\par
Open the upper-right hamburger menu, choose Copy Requests, then choose New Request. Select one Request File (.txt) and one Destination folder. Both fields support Browse; on native Windows they also accept drag/drop. The request file uses the same comments as toBeInventoried.txt: blank lines, # comment lines, REM, and REM followed by text are ignored. A request line can be a complete Show Name, an Artist, Artist plus a calendar year (yyyy), Artist plus an exact yyyy-mm-dd date, or Artist plus a yyyy-yyyy/yy-yy range. Examples: Grateful Dead; Grateful Dead 1977; Grateful Dead 1977-05-08; Grateful Dead 1972-1978; Grateful Dead 99-01; Grateful Dead 1977-05-08 Barton Hall Ithaca, NY. The range 99-01 means 1999 through 2001.\par
\par
Artist requests use Artist DB masters and aliases. A broad Artist request selects every matching unique Show in the inventory; Artist plus date selects every matching Show on that exact date; a range selects every matching Show in the inclusive years. Complete Show Name matching is exact apart from case and insignificant whitespace. Overlapping request lines never cause the same Show to be copied twice. If the same Show exists on several inventoried source volumes, TLO uses one currently connected source and still records only one completed Show.\par
\par
The Copy Requests window lists every saved request separately. Two list files are two independent requests. The same list sent to a different Destination is also a separate request. Continue rechecks the current inventory and currently connected source volumes, so a request automatically discovers matching Shows inventoried after an earlier pass. If the original request file changed, TLO asks whether to Update Request from the changed file or Continue with its saved copy. If the original file is unavailable, the saved request can still continue.\par
\par
Preview distinguishes Available on connected volumes, Waiting For Volumes, stale/missing source folders on connected volumes, and Unmatched request items. Waiting is not a failed search. Preview also lists which disconnected volume labels would satisfy the most remaining Shows so you can choose which drives to connect next.\par
\par
Before copying, TLO totals every valid folder planned for that request in the current pass and checks Destination free space. If the entire current pass does not fit, TLO copies nothing for that request. Each later pass gets a new full free-space check. Source folders are never renamed, retagged, moved, or deleted. Copy Requests reject source trees containing symbolic links, copy into a temporary Destination folder, verify the copied tree, then rename the verified temporary folder to the source folder's original leaf name. TLO never merges with or overwrites a different existing Destination folder. If another request already copied an exact byte-for-byte folder to the same Destination, the later request records that Show as Already Satisfied instead of copying it again.\par
\par
Process All Active Requests runs every request that is not Closed against the source volumes connected now. Each request performs its own free-space preflight because different requests may use different Destinations. View Report shows the latest detailed result. Close Request changes the user-chosen terminal state to Closed; the request remains visible for history but will no longer be processed. Delete Request deletes only TLO's saved request state and reports and never deletes copied music.\par
\par
Automatic states are In Progress, Waiting For Volumes, Matches Complete / Unmatched Remain, and Complete. Complete can become active again for a broad Artist or range request if later inventory adds another matching Show. Closed is the only user-chosen terminal state. TLO stores request state and reports under TLOHome/copyRequests. Each request keeps cumulative copied.txt plus current pending.txt and failed.txt, latest-report.txt, and timestamped pass reports.\par
\par
\b Build 486 Copy Requests menu placement\b0\par
Build 486 removes the Copy Requests button from the main action row and places Copy Requests under the upper-right hamburger menu. Selecting it opens the same Copy Requests manager; no Copy Request workflow or other GUI layout/behavior changes are introduced.\par
\par
\b Build 487 Redundancy Groups for backup/equivalent volumes\b0\par
Open the upper-right hamburger menu and choose Redundancy Groups to tell Copy Requests that two or more volume labels are equivalent replicas. Enter one group per line using equals signs, in the exact order TLO should prefer them. Example: Juke3 = Back-up3 = Back-up3a. If Juke3 and Back-up3a are both connected, TLO uses Juke3. If Juke3 is unavailable but Back-up3 is available, TLO uses Back-up3. The leftmost member always has the highest precedence and the rightmost member the lowest.\par
You may enter a visible volume label or a currently accessible volume/root path for each member. Paths with spaces do not require quotes. When you Save, path entries are resolved to their visible volume labels and the saved declaration is normalized to labels. A path must be accessible at Save time so TLO can resolve its label; if it is not connected, enter the known volume label instead.\par
You only need to inventory one copy. If bootlist.csv says a Show is on Juke3 at /G/Grateful Dead/1977/show, and Back-up3a is connected instead, Copy Requests checks that same rootless path on Back-up3a. If the folder exists, the backup can satisfy the request. Copy Request reports show both the inventoried volume and the actual equivalent volume used.\par
A volume label may belong to only one Redundancy Group. Redundancy Groups do not add backup copies to bootlist.csv and do not change ordinary duplicate handling. They are used only to locate interchangeable physical sources for Copy Requests.\par
